Q4. SQL Concepts (4 Marks)
1. Why create indexes, and when should they be avoided?
Indexes speed up SELECT queries, joins, filtering and sorting. Avoid excessive indexes on tables with frequent inserts,
updates or deletes because indexes use storage and increase write-maintenance costs.
2. WHERE vs HAVING
WHERE HAVING
Filters rows before grouping. Filters groups after GROUP BY.
Usually uses row-level conditions. Often filters aggregate values.
-- WHERE example
SELECT DepartmentId, AVG(Salary) AS AverageSalary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary > 30000
GROUP BY DepartmentId;
-- HAVING example
SELECT DepartmentId, AVG(Salary) AS AverageSalary
FROM Employees
GROUP BY DepartmentId
HAVING AVG(Salary) > 50000;
3. Clustered vs non-clustered index
A clustered index organizes table rows by its key; a table can have only one. A non-clustered index stores keys and row
locators separately from the data; a table can have multiple.
CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX IX_Employees_EmployeeId
ON Employees(EmployeeId);
CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Employees_Name
ON Employees(Name);
The clustered-index command assumes the table does not already have one.
4. What happens when COMMIT and ROLLBACK execute?
COMMIT: completes the transaction, makes its changes permanent, and releases applicable locks. ROLLBACK: undoes
uncommitted changes using transaction log information and releases applicable locks when rollback completes.
