Question 4 — Inventory Transaction, SQL and EF Core (5 Marks)
a) Why a transaction is required
A transaction ensures all order operations succeed together or fail together. ACID means Atomicity (all or nothing),
Consistency (rules remain valid), Isolation (concurrent orders do not consume the same stock incorrectly), and
Durability (committed changes remain saved).
b) SQL Server transaction for placing an order
SET XACT_ABORT ON;
BEGIN TRY
 BEGIN TRANSACTION;
 DECLARE @AvailableStock INT;
 SELECT @AvailableStock = StockQuantity
 FROM Products WITH (UPDLOCK, HOLDLOCK)
 WHERE ProductId = @ProductId;
 IF @AvailableStock IS NULL
 THROW 50001, 'Product not found.', 1;
 IF @AvailableStock < @Quantity
 THROW 50002, 'Insufficient stock.', 1;
 UPDATE Products
 SET StockQuantity = StockQuantity - @Quantity
 WHERE ProductId = @ProductId
 AND StockQuantity >= @Quantity;
 IF @@ROWCOUNT = 0
 THROW 50003, 'Insufficient stock.', 1;
 INSERT INTO Orders (CustomerId, OrderDate, TotalAmount)
 VALUES (@CustomerId, GETDATE(), @TotalAmount);
 DECLARE @OrderId INT = SCOPE_IDENTITY();
 INSERT INTO OrderItems (OrderId, ProductId, Quantity, Price)
 VALUES (@OrderId, @ProductId, @Quantity, @Price);
 COMMIT TRANSACTION;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
 IF XACT_STATE() <> 0
 ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;
 THROW;
END CATCH;
Declare @ProductId, @Quantity, @CustomerId, @TotalAmount, and @Price as parameters or local variables. For multiple
products, update all items in the same transaction and use a consistent locking strategy.
The product row lock prevents another transaction from consuming the same last unit while the first transaction is processing. The
conditional UPDATE and row-count check provide an additional stock safeguard.
