Question 5 — Production API Failure Investigation (4 Marks)
1. SQL indexing
Create indexes on frequently filtered and joined columns, such as DepartmentId, Status, and suitable email lookup
columns. Avoid unnecessary indexes that slow writes.
2. Query optimization and execution plans
Select only required columns, use suitable joins, avoid N+1 queries, and inspect SQL Server execution plans for scans
and expensive operations.
3. Pagination
Use Skip/Take or keyset pagination and enforce a maximum page size to reduce memory and bandwidth.
4. Async programming
Use async/await with EF Core methods such as ToListAsync and SaveChangesAsync so request threads are not
blocked while waiting for database operations.
5. Caching
Cache suitable repeated reads with IMemoryCache or distributed cache such as Redis. Invalidate or update cached
data when employees change.
6. Global exception handling
Use exception-handling middleware and standardized ProblemDetails responses. Log details internally; do not expose
stack traces to clients.
7. Structured logging
Use ILogger, request correlation IDs, query-duration logging, and monitoring tools such as OpenTelemetry or
Application Insights to trace slow requests and SQL.
8. File validation
Limit upload size, verify allowed image formats and file signatures, generate safe filenames, and resize images when
appropriate.
9. Rate limiting
Configure ASP.NET Core rate limiting with suitable per-user or per-client limits to reduce overload and abuse.
