Question 2 — SQL Design and Optimization (5 Marks)
Given query
SELECT *
FROM Employee
WHERE DepartmentId = 5
 AND Salary > 50000
ORDER BY JoiningDate DESC;
1. Why is it slow?
Possible reasons: the table contains many records; SQL Server may scan the table; suitable indexes may be missing; SELECT
* retrieves unnecessary columns; sorting by JoiningDate may require an expensive sort; and the filters may match many rows.
2. Which index would you create?
CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Employee_Department_Salary_JoiningDate
ON Employee (DepartmentId, Salary, JoiningDate DESC);
This can help filtering and reduce reads, but because Salary is a range condition, the index may not fully satisfy the
JoiningDate ordering. Test alternatives with actual execution plans and representative data.
3. Rewrite the query for better performance
SELECT EmployeeId, Name, DepartmentId, Salary, JoiningDate
FROM Employee
WHERE DepartmentId = 5
 AND Salary > 50000
ORDER BY JoiningDate DESC;
Selecting only required columns reduces unnecessary data retrieval; the filtering and sorting logic remains unchanged.
