3. Clustered and non-clustered indexes
A clustered index organizes table data rows by the index key; a table can have only one. A non-clustered index stores keys
and row locators separately from table data; a table can have multiple.
CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX IX_Employee_Id ON Employee(EmployeeId);
CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Employee_Name ON Employee(Name);
The clustered-index command assumes the table does not already have a clustered index.
4. What is a deadlock? How can it be prevented?
A deadlock occurs when transactions wait for resources held by one another. SQL Server normally detects it and chooses one
transaction as the victim. Prevent deadlocks by accessing resources in a consistent order, keeping transactions short, indexing
appropriately, avoiding unnecessary locks, and retrying deadlock victims with a limited retry policy.
